{"title":"2010 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 13","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Algebra)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Algebra)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Quick)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Quick)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6(Algebra)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6(Algebra)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7_(Trig)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7 (Trig)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For how many integer values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> do the graphs of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/0/b806a23f9776fe081ff9a88053326282f59e4c07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2=k^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/8/be8079964c914a52c7786cbc8d787c0936dfb558.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy = k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" /> not intersect?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/d/48de382bbcd29f32313ab2f62bd96c0456e142e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 0 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ 1 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ 2 \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 4 \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"348\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The image below shows the two curves for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/a/eeae143c5c9dbd376ffa1ffa8bdb93a58e8c9889.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />. The blue curve is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/0/b806a23f9776fe081ff9a88053326282f59e4c07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2=k^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />, which is clearly a circle with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, and the red curve is a part of the curve <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f8bfc4a69f98f57e935422dd60a7e821b9a634.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy=k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/b/5bbace743a2cf4f73b734616d0b3bde5f326531d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import graph; size(200);  real f(real x) {return 4/x;}; real g1(real x) {return sqrt(4*4-x*x);}; real g2(real x) {return -sqrt(4*4-x*x);}; draw(graph(f,-20./3,-0.6),red); draw(graph(f,0.6,20./3),red); draw(graph(g1,-4,4),blue); draw(graph(g2,-4,4),blue); axes(&quot;$x$&quot;,&quot;$y$&quot;); [/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"335\" />\n</p><p>In the special case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/d/badf0ecd1ef8289387d8462393438c6e2710ff28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> the blue curve is just the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />, and as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/e/35e722cebe029667c1ffbe7fec74c002ef4eddc3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\cdot 0=0$\" width=\"64\" height=\"12\" />, this point is on the red curve as well, hence they intersect. \n</p><p>The case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/b/f0b043119262a2beb49176cb4beb5c50ec1915a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&lt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> is symmetric to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/e/67e6630318d8cb28de2723180b6d359b972489c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />: the blue curve remains the same and the red curve is flipped according to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> axis. Hence we just need to focus on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/e/67e6630318d8cb28de2723180b6d359b972489c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Clearly, on the red curve there will always be points arbitrarily far from the origin: for example, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> approaches 0, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> approaches <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/7/b671f1bb7e4ee86584347d5d22f1dc8abdb5bef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"9\" />. Hence the red curve intersects the blue one if and only if it contains a point whose distance from the origin is at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nAt this point we can guess that on the red curve the point where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/1/0110f1bb04c51b2a4d431bf14489fc1b48b37421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"11\" /> is always closest to the origin, and skip the rest of this solution.\n</p><p><br />\nFor an exact solution, fix <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> and consider any point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> on the red curve. Its distance from the origin is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/b/18b7589940b7fe89ad8b7c74f96819443fb3f63e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{ x^2 + (k/x)^2 }$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"109\" height=\"32\" />. To minimize this distance, it is enough to minimize <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/9/c2914b55cd63fb5a2d0aceeefbe4c3936c499a0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + (k/x)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"19\" />. By the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Arithmetic_Mean-Geometric_Mean_Inequality\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality\">Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality</a> we get that this value is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/e/b8ea7ee8a282949fd853a01537ec7a2b55e56f75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2k$\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" />, and that equality holds whenever <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/1/081605f65e25ffefd15e5fb38868cf3c3f0b2223.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 = (k/x)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"19\" />, i.e., <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/f/b7fccd77d5fc072fb2ec825c4c5958d3375fc138.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\pm\\sqrt k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nNow recall that the red curve intersects the blue one if and only if its closest point is at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> from the origin. We just computed that the distance between the origin and the closest point on the red curve is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/7/0374f2bf7b18fd0cfe04a4cf8fd633d4ea952e25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore, we want to find all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/5/34592017749664e415d710d5f80938bb6b928b3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2k} &gt; k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Clearly the only such integer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/d/5ed8470bc717a49aafe4556c7e2c2e11ddc7b687.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, hence the two curves are only disjoint for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/d/5ed8470bc717a49aafe4556c7e2c2e11ddc7b687.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/9/db97681e49591a8b330c0dfa9907ee21c2feca2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" />. \nThis is a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/5/dc541507a8109c2fec31fc026137e62ee2c9375d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{2\\ \\textbf{(C)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"55\" height=\"28\" /> values.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>From the graph shown above, we see that there is a specific point closest to the center of the circle. Using some logic, we realize that as long as said furthest point is not inside or on the graph of the circle. This should be enough to conclude that the hyperbola does not intersect the circle. \n</p><p>Therefore, for each value of k, we only need to check said value to determine intersection. Let said point, closest to the circle have coordinates <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/4/bc43f63db5b439939251890fbb979ecbd5ef5de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, k/x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> derived from the equation. Then, all coordinates that satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cdafa2a2fad4dba429186889b841454febb49b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{ x^2+ (k/x)^2 } \\leq k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"142\" height=\"32\" /> intersect the circle.\nSquaring, we find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/8/8e86003bed608f0dcee7c91cff4f828310c3ffeb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+(k/x)^2 \\leq k^2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"19\" />\nAfter multiplying through by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b2878564ab19b80abb21ba964abe90fe120846.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2$\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> and rearranging, we find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/8/1e80867fd0cd1046a80e04b99107dac93b165673.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^4-x^2k^2+k^2 \\leq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"148\" height=\"17\" />.\nWe see this is a quadratic in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b2878564ab19b80abb21ba964abe90fe120846.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2$\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" /> and consider taking the determinant, which tells us that solutions are real when, after factoring:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/2/4f2211bd2ddbc3ff094ed760688c8e8b880fe590.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k^2(k^2-4) \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"19\" />\nWe plot this inequality on the number line to find it is satisfied for all values except: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/8/1d84d431f5b6541216a7c475c4af0d2a7b3b7f09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-1, 0, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" />.\nWe then eliminate 0 because it is extraneous as both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/1/f71ba6fae7a17cf36f35b44715a9ab362fe293e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/5/b95be149fe43e267c27024cbe13fd852cb0b78c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> are points which coincide.\nTherefore, there are a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/5/dc541507a8109c2fec31fc026137e62ee2c9375d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{2\\ \\textbf{(C)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"55\" height=\"28\" /> values.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Algebra.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Algebra)\">Solution 3 (Algebra)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Algebra)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f8bfc4a69f98f57e935422dd60a7e821b9a634.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy=k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" />, multiply the equation by 2 on both sides to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/c/30cd3464d65e208c2e78628f8166f0c87e1e1308.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2xy=2k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"16\" />. Now we can add the two equations to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/0/1308e13be7e8bf00baa670593f9e70521e24034f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x+y)^2=k^2+2k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"19\" />, for which the only value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> that does not satisfy the equation is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />, as that makes the RHS negative. Similarly, if we subtract the two equations, we obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/d/2bd9280fb57bde843469f5d9ed5fa2fa3d4b09a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x-y)^2=k^2-2k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"19\" />, for which the only value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> that does not satisfy the equation is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, for the same reason above.\n</p><p>Thus, the only values are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/4/e344fac2a66988779fea1386f6216f003d0e6324.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 1, -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"73\" height=\"16\" />, giving us a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/5/dc541507a8109c2fec31fc026137e62ee2c9375d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{2\\ \\textbf{(C)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"55\" height=\"28\" /> values.\n</p><p>~ ccx09 (Roy Short)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Quick.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Quick)\">Solution 4 (Quick)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Quick)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Multiply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/e/45e38963d51c32fba608b10b17a787983a622303.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> and substitute it into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/b/91b7a5958b8e97ffb4cebe4b952355946b16bec2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k^2=x^2+y^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/4/b94f2041c4015bdfca581bc3b17af6e7d03b76dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=\\frac{x^2+y^2}{xy}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"94\" height=\"42\" />. Recognize it? It's also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/5/6159d35f3eec622856b4e0b1fda7f53d55a023f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"83\" height=\"36\" />. The minimum of this function (more accurately the minimum absolute value of the function) is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/a/a4a013a066554da8372fba5fb2b29812c46b80c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=2, -2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> (when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/1/0110f1bb04c51b2a4d431bf14489fc1b48b37421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"11\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/c/52c6f440a0a502b9742ddfe66649ed66b834d8ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=-y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"57\" height=\"11\" />). As long as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/7/c877cdefa36f90cdf80f93dadc93cc77d19961a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/f/7ffaed8dd2936d01acee7f2c91ec9c551a9acdd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&lt;-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"59\" height=\"16\" />, the function is valid. As such, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6e8cff7a226d1910166f545db155d88a6dc44b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\neq1,-1 \\implies \\boxed{2\\ \\textbf{(C)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"179\" height=\"28\" />. Elegant, huh? Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba4eb8fa1d9bafe6435b56c868b6facd16d0c7c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-2&lt;0&lt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"92\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> must also not be valid so why was <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> not considered?\n</p><p>~~BJHHar\n</p><p>~~~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Jadon_jung\" title=\"User:Jadon jung\">SnappyRiffs</a> (<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User_talk:Jadon_jung\" title=\"User talk:Jadon jung\">talk</a>)(minor edits;comment: solution was incomplete-this can lead to confusion)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/b/c5b89241aaba4a80dcc173ca070c6de1d083f62e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> since if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> works then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/d/bfd84c64e9f58c9dd52ce680ab4d70f5dc17b5e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-k$\" width=\"23\" height=\"12\" /> also works. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/5/5552a6f39c8678b12542c44050f8db460fe0164e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = ka$\" width=\"54\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/d/a5dca081353a26c8170e52fa33b4f22ea14bea26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = kb$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />. Then the given equations become <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/7/c578306a7c8d25a825e4db62889e4da29f79dc9e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ab = \\frac{1}{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"58\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/d/65d748c7c6bf4654ee4f39134fd4d11db81d2942.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2 + b^2 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"87\" height=\"16\" />, which we don't want to intersect. The points that are the closest on this graph are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/2/5729a010140afe237d93d750a070091011bbbfcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1/\\sqrt{2}, 1/\\sqrt{2})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"109\" height=\"24\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/0/ee0128acefa2d266de723669a3228cf9b11676d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1/\\sqrt{k})(1/\\sqrt{k})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"117\" height=\"24\" />. We don't want the hyperbola to intersect or go inside the circle, so we require <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/d/96dc836d03489a031a1367ca81204d045c567e7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/\\sqrt{k} &gt; 1/\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"24\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/c/4dcc8f1a4b2ec2a3b52c18b02f8237880088cbfb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&lt; 2\\implies k = 0, 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"155\" height=\"18\" />. Also, by symmetry <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/2/a82f7b76d2e16054f75df1ea45cb2a043f09ffad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"59\" height=\"16\" /> also works.Obviously <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/84574de7797bc7b4e1fc52075f4d265ba60a8bce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> doesn't work, so we can discard that, leaving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/30291216a204224f18043b4da99d1405b9bcb2f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathbf{(C)}\\quad 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"68\" height=\"32\" /> integral solutions for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6.28Algebra.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6(Algebra)\">Solution 6(Algebra)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6(Algebra)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To know when the system does not intersect, we first need to find when they do intersect. To do so, we substitute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f8bfc4a69f98f57e935422dd60a7e821b9a634.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy=k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" /> into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/d/23d35351f223cc356dc6176f444648c4c7f587fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2}+y^{2}=k^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" /> giving:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/f/4cf54e5264d48ee667c049ae7a681b9fb778de47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2=(xy)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"125\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>By Simon's Favorite Factoring Trick, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/6/7e6ad237e47bcdb9b3ec3a94299e41849361bf2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1=(1-x)(1+x)(1-y)(1+y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"257\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Notice that other than for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and/or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> and/or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> because for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> we can get some coordinates that work even if they're fractions which through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f8bfc4a69f98f57e935422dd60a7e821b9a634.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy=k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" /> it can become an integer as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/d/e2d14d09d779e7999cdb06fc94d59eb2a11617cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{2}\\times2=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"80\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>This means we have to exclude <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/0/cb0aa983fa93718a322667f131355f65f22026d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy=k=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />. This is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/e/b5e8656291085c388b48a0fa8da85aed21c483bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"20\" height=\"23\" /> values that do not work.\n</p><p>~Batmanstark\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7_.28Trig.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7_(Trig)\">Solution 7 (Trig)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7 (Trig)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The first equation is describing a circle with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />, so we can rewrite it in terms of a new variable <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/3/72370c510a39fef3a388b2aa19496848d9298c13.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(k\\cos(\\theta))^2+(k\\sin(\\theta))^2=k^2.\\]\" width=\"228\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>A quick check shows that this identity holds since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/f/aaf4dfd4ee442874baf3d6e0391f24d44114aaf2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin^2+\\cos^2=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"117\" height=\"19\" />. Now, with an alternate expression for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, we can plug this into the second equation to get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e538fa11f4221398e89aa9dc353bb9d222114a19.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(k\\sin(\\theta))(k\\cos(\\theta))=k\\rightarrow k^2\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)=k.\\]\" width=\"363\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Since the two equations does intersect when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/d/badf0ecd1ef8289387d8462393438c6e2710ff28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/0/9d0338cdad4134e835145541ef1f0bc401796a94.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)=(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"18\" />, we do not need to consider the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/d/badf0ecd1ef8289387d8462393438c6e2710ff28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> case, so we can safely divide both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/5/0e5c83cc0c639fc2ed843bbe2d3d209fb478b104.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" /> to get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93103a4f43bb4887271c39183c80e9c1c44e287.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)=\\frac{1}{k}.\\]\" width=\"137\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>We can see that the intersections of these two equations will remain unchanged, only now that the solutions will be in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Because the range of both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/b/6fb300dacfcde1b57ad070409262b1743e0cd966.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/5/bb5e108fdc4bf4fb6151cb922992f23bc6c35985.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos$\" width=\"24\" height=\"8\" /> are limited to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e861e10e1c19918756b9c8b7717684593c63aeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />, the range of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/0/3b087294fffdf6c50005254d0038570171b14bf8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"98\" height=\"20\" /> will not exceed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. However, the only way that the product could equal 1 is if both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ea19187c412da5e82c8ca0aacda5402d14ae7e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(\\theta)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/7/42781e6e22b30f45c904fcccdaffa1124d6a2d77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos(\\theta)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"18\" /> to equal 1 at the same time, so since the maxima of the functions don't match up, this is an impossible case. Therefore, this equation will only have valid solutions when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/f/08ff8ea020546fb6e8c90abbfb0b50802eee802d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, not at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/d/5ed8470bc717a49aafe4556c7e2c2e11ddc7b687.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />. A quick sanity check with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/c/73cb965828218ea9ad0e13ae0c094bba8588b9e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"50\" height=\"34\" /> shows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/0/d6059b5bcb29f265e0522c51c32ea11bda6de693.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{k}=\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"53\" height=\"38\" /> and all values less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> is indeed contained in the range.\n</p><p>For all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/b/f0b043119262a2beb49176cb4beb5c50ec1915a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&lt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />, symmetry about the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />-axis will give an identical argument with the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/e/07e1c2225d1b6735aa9a9990b66f7e298831457f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)=-\\frac{1}{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"150\" height=\"38\" />, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/2/a724d4b0354645cfa79653dce6cc013649cd5815.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&lt;-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"59\" height=\"16\" /> producing valid solutions for our system. Then, the only two values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that the system of equations won't intersect are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />, a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/e/b5e8656291085c388b48a0fa8da85aed21c483bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"20\" height=\"23\" /> values.\n</p><p>(Note: In the graph of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/4/014f78f33984d5265b3a99fc58ff821753467a6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)=\\sin(x)\\cos(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"160\" height=\"20\" />, the range of the function is actually even more restricted, being <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/4/7b4d4d27b83ac913c3db573832adfdd83600986a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"69\" height=\"45\" />. However, in a contest, observing that the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/6/ae60b1d3d2da219c83a342ebd15cab730efa1e5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\sin(x)\\cos(x)|&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"147\" height=\"20\" /> would suffice since the question is only asking for integer values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />.)\n</p><p>~chz3369\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0YwShc-9n-4\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0YwShc-9n-4</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A\" title=\"2010 AMC 12A\">2010 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2010 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2010 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2010 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?titl